# The box's scheduled work, rendered to cron.d/ond by `mise run deploy:api` and
# installed to /etc/cron.d/ond by the same task.
#
# Installed by deploy rather than written by cloud-init, and that is the whole
# reason this file exists here. cloud-init runs once, at first boot, and never
# again: an instance keeps the crontab it was born with, so a fix to the backup
# reaches a fresh box and no other. That is not theoretical either — first-boot
# drift is how the nightly dump pointed at the wrong database for eight days.
# A file that ships with every deploy cannot drift from the repository.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/snap/bin
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=__REGION__

# 02:17 rather than 02:00 — nothing else runs here, but an off-the-hour minute
# costs nothing and keeps this out of the way of anything added later.
#
# Output goes to the journal rather than a file in /var/log: journald already
# rotates, and an unrotated log on the root volume is the disk-fill this whole
# change is trying to make visible. Read it back with `journalctl -t ond-backup`.
#
# The pipe means cron sees logger's exit status rather than the script's, which
# would matter if cron's status were the signal. It is not: backup.sh writes
# ond_backup_success and the last-success timestamp to the textfile collector,
# and BackupStale reads those. The metric is the report; this is the transcript.
17 2 * * * root /usr/local/bin/backup.sh __BACKUP_BUCKET__ 2>&1 | logger -t ond-backup

# Five minutes against a CloudWatch alarm that treats missing data as breaching.
# Frequent enough that a dead monitoring stack is noticed inside a coffee break,
# infrequent enough to stay far inside the CloudWatch free tier.
*/5 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/heartbeat.sh __HEARTBEAT_NAMESPACE__ __HEARTBEAT_METRIC__ 2>&1 | logger -t ond-heartbeat
